---
title: Chocolate Chip Cookies
description: Large chocolate chip cookies from dough rested a full day, finished with sea salt.
tags: [cookies, baking]
course: dessert
source:
  name: The New York Times
  url: https://www.nytimes.com/2008/07/09/dining/091crex.html
adapted: true
yield: 31-34%cookies
time: 24h45m
---

Sift the @cake flour{241%g}(2 cups minus 2 tsp), @bread flour{241%g}(1 2/3 cups), @baking soda{1 1/4%tsp}, @baking powder{1 1/2%tsp} and @coarse salt{1 1/2%tsp} into a #bowl{}. Set aside.

Using a #mixer{} fitted with the paddle attachment, cream the @unsalted butter{280%g}(2 1/2 sticks, 1 1/4 cups), @light brown sugar{284%g}(1 1/4 cups) and @granulated sugar{227%g}(1 cup plus 2 tbsp) together until very light, about ~cream{5%minutes}.

Add the @eggs{2}(large), one at a time, mixing well after each addition. Stir in the @natural vanilla extract{2%tsp}.

Reduce the speed to low, add the dry ingredients and mix until just combined, ~mix{5-10%seconds}.

Drop the @bittersweet chocolate{567%g}(1 1/4 lb, disks or chips, at least 60% cacao) pieces in and incorporate them without breaking them.

Press #plastic wrap{} against the dough and refrigerate for ~chill{24-36%hours}. The dough may be used in batches, and can be refrigerated for up to 72 hours.

When ready to bake, preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Line a #baking sheet{} with #parchment paper{}(or a nonstick baking mat). Set aside.

Scoop 6–9 mounds of dough (2 oz each) onto the baking sheet, making sure to turn horizontally any chocolate pieces that are poking up; it will make for a more attractive cookie. Sprinkle lightly with @coarse sea salt{}(or coarse kosher salt).

> Original size of cookies was recommended to be 3.5 oz, but 2 oz yields much more cookies and the size is still large and retains all textures wanted from this cookie.

Bake until the surface is cracked and the edges are golden brown but the cookie is still soft, ~bake{18-20%minutes}.

Transfer the sheet to a #wire rack{} for ~cool{10%minutes}, then slip the cookies onto another rack to cool a bit more.

Repeat with the remaining dough, or reserve the dough, refrigerated, for baking remaining batches the next day.
